      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "d1f51356-f473-5e33-8480-34340290d801",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr.; Ben Sira; Joshua son of Sirach\n- Works: Sirach; Ecclesiasticus; Ben Sira Scroll\n- Places: Masada; Palestine; Jerusalem\n- Concepts: Biblical Hebrew; Aramaic; Syriac; Hellenistic Greek; critical Hebrew text\n\nFacts:\n- Sirach, also called the Wisdom of Sirach, Ecclesiasticus, or Ben Sira, is a work of ethical teachings written around 200 to 175 BCE.\n- Ben Sira of Jerusalem wrote Sirach, and he is also identified as Joshua son of Sirach, Jesus son of Sirach, or Yeshua ben Eliezer ben Sira.\n- Portions of Sirach are from a first-century B.C. book.\n- In 1964, the Ben Sira Scroll was discovered at Masada in Palestine.\n- Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr. was an American scholar with knowledge of Biblical Hebrew, Aramaic, Syriac, and Hellenistic Greek.\n- Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr. analyzed the text of the Ben Sira Scroll discovered at Masada.\n- Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr.'s research established a critical Hebrew text for portions of Sirach.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "fbe6878b-8275-53ed-924e-b5135f4272df",
        "text": "Page: Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr.. Sentence: Dr. Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr. was an American scholar whose knowledge of Biblical Hebrew, Aramaic, Syriac and Hellenistic Greek enabled him to undertake an analysis of the text of the Ben Sira Scroll discovered at Masada in Palestine in 1964.\nPage: Roy Kinneer Patteson, Jr.. Sentence: His research resulted in the establishment of a critical Hebrew text for portions of the first-century B.C. book, Sirach.\nPage: Sirach. Sentence: The Book of the All-Virtuous Wisdom of Joshua ben Sira, commonly called the Wisdom of Sirach or simply Sirach, and also known as the Book of Ecclesiasticus (abbreviated Ecclus.)\nPage: Sirach. Sentence: or Ben Sira, is a work of ethical teachings, from approximately 200 to 175 BCE, written by the Jewish scribe Ben Sira of Jerusalem, on the inspiration of his father Joshua son of Sirach, sometimes called Jesus son of Sirach or Yeshua ben Eliezer ben Sira.",
